  {
    "id": "cvpr26-433c6807f4ab",
    "extraction": {
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1 : Reasoning pattern comparison. (a) Greedy decoding: the base VLM selects the top-1 token at each step; any hallucination in an intermediate step propagates to an incorrect final answer.\n(b) RLHF-based “think-with-images”: the model learns when to call tools to zoom or crop the image and re-inject cropped regions into the reasoning context—effective but costly and model-specific.\n(c) Ours: a lightweight, training-free, model-agnostic framework. A supervisor maintains a dynamic visual-evidence pool to detect and correct hallucination steps. When uncertainty arises, it invokes a visual decider to extract new evidence, enabling visually grounded reasoning throughout the chain.",
      "image_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2602.21497v2/Fig1.png",
      "figureAnchor": "S0.F1",
      "panelIndex": 0,
