    "extraction": {
      "figure": "Figure 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2 : Method Overview of FlexAvatar . Given the single input image I I , our method allows to change both viewpoint π \\pi and facial expression z e ​ x ​ p z_{exp} . The transformer-based encoder E E first produces a compressed avatar code 𝒜 \\mathcal{A} via cross-attention. The decoder D D then incorporates the effect of the facial expression z e ​ x ​ p z_{exp} into the avatar representation. Crucially, the corresponding bias sinks are concatenated to the expression tokens: z 2 ​ D z_{2D} if the input image I I comes from a monocular dataset, and z 3 ​ D z_{3D} if it comes from a multi-view dataset. Finally, the upsampled avatar code is decoded into the 3D Gaussian attributes for rendering. During training, the bias sinks absorb data modality-specific biases such as the entanglement of driver expression and target viewpoint of monocular datasets. At inference time, only z 3 ​ D z_{3D} is used to inherit the disentangled behavior of multi-view datasets yielding both generalized and complete 3D head avatars.",
      "image_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15599v2/3_method_overview.png",
      "figureAnchor": "S2.F2",
